#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151830 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca68460-f3fe-5ae8-b5f4-85742c0ef46b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Wronskian of Two Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "3da98f86-afab-5b4c-b51a-2ebb42248da1" :question/math-academy-id "e-21680" :question/problem "Compute the Wronskian $W(y_1, y_2)$ for $y_1(x) =2x$ and $y_2(x) = x^2$." :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of two functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $y_1(x) = 2x$ and\n\n$$\ny_2(x) = x^2\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= 2x(x^{2})^{′} - x^{2}(2x)^{′} \\\\\n&= 4x^{2} - 2x^{2} \\\\\n&= 2x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151831 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd428707-bb32-58b2-8a51-09e330af0bb1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Independent Functions Using the Wronskian"}] :question/id #uuid "7e72c2e1-8bbd-5047-9394-5df205a9c85a" :question/math-academy-id "e-21681" :question/problem "$y_1(x) = x^2 + 1, \\qquad y_2(x) = 2x, \\qquad x \\in \\mathbb R$\nConsider the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ above. Given that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the same second-order linear differential equation, use the Wronskian to explain why the set $\\{y_1(x), y_2(x) \\}$ is linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ are defined on some interval $I$.\nThe Wronskian $W(y_1,y_2)$ of these functions is defined as\n$\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}$\n\nRecall the following:\n\n- If $W \\neq 0$ for at least one $x \\in I$, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly independent.\n- If $W = 0$ and it’s known that $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions to the same second-order linear differential equation, then $y_1$ and $y_2$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, the Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nW(y_{1}, y_{2}) &= \\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} + 1 & 2x \\\\ (x^{2} + 1)^{′} & (2x)^{′}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}x^{2} + 1 & 2x \\\\ 2x & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (x^{2} + 1) \\cdot 2 - (2x) \\cdot (2x) \\\\\n&= 2(x^{2} + 1) - 4x^{2} \\\\\n&= 2x^{2} + 2 - 4x^{2} \\\\\n&= 2 - 2x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is nonzero for at least one $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\nTherefore, the set\n\n$$\n\\left\\{y_1(x), y_2(x) \\right\\}\n$$\n\nis linearly independent on $\\mathbb R$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151832 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e66005fe-df33-590a-9d9c-91947cd4a608" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Fundamental Sets of Solutions"}] :question/id #uuid "c330ca05-3b63-5525-b51e-24d0926a359c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21682" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $x^2y'' - 5x y' + 9y=0$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y_1(x) = x^3$ is a solution to the equation for $x\\in \\mathbb R$.\n2. $y_2(x) = x^3 \\ln x$ is a solution to the equation for $x > 0$.\n3. The functions $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation.\n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_1(x){:}$\n$y_{1}^{′}(x)|= 3x^{2}; y_{1}^{″}(x)|= 6x$\nNow, let's substitute $y_1(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n$x^{2} \\cdot 6x - 5x \\cdot 3x^{2} + 9x^{3}|= 0; 6x^{3} - 15x^{3} + 9x^{3}|= 0; 0|= 0✓$\nSo, $y_1(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of $y_2(x){:}$\n$y_{2}^{′}(x)|= 3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}; y_{2}^{″}(x)|= 6x\\ln x + 5x$\nNow, let's substitute $y_2(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n$x^{2}(6x\\ln x + 5x) - 5x(3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}) + 9x^{3}\\ln x|= 0; (6x^{3}\\ln x + 5x^{3} - 15x^{3}\\ln x - 5x^{3} + 9x^{3}\\ln x)|= 0; 0|= 0✓$\nSo, $y_2(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n- Statement III is true. Both $y_1$ and $y_2$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n$W(y_{1}, y_{2})|=\\begin{vmatrix}y_{1} & y_{2} \\\\ y_{1}^{′} & y_{2}^{′}\\end{vmatrix}; =\\begin{vmatrix}x^{3} & x^{3}\\ln x \\\\ 3x^{2} & 3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}\\end{vmatrix}; = x^{3}(3x^{2}\\ln x + x^{2}) - x^{3}\\ln x \\cdot 3x^{2}; = 3x^{5}\\ln x + x^{5} - 3x^{5}\\ln x; = x^{5}$\nSince the Wronskian is not identically $0$ on $(0, \\infty)$, the functions are linearly independent. Hence, $y_1(x)$ and $y_2(x)$ form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation on $(0, \\infty)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151833 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe4c8bb4-b7c0-5afc-86fd-40c20df9b953" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Dependent and Linearly Independent Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "708d2d6d-df17-57e3-bff2-ee8809cf9e3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21683" :question/problem "Given the set of functions $\\{e^x, \\, \\sin x \\}$ defined on $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, explain why they are linearly independent." :question/worked-solution "Recall that the set of functions $\\{f_1(x), f_2(x), \\ldots, f_n(x) \\}$, which are defined on some interval $I$, is *linearly dependent* if there exist real scalars $k_1,k_2,\\ldots,k_n$ not all zero such that the linear combination $k_1 \\cdot f_1 + k_2 \\cdot f_2 + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2$ is identically zero, i.e,\n\n$$\nk_1 \\cdot f_1(x) + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) + \\ldots + k_2 \\cdot f_2(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x \\in I$. Otherwise, if\n\n$$\nk_1 = k_2 = \\cdots = k_n = 0\n$$\n\nis the only solution, the set is *linearly independent*.\n\nNow, suppose for all $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, we have that\n\n$$\nk_1 e^x + k_2 \\sin x = 0\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- If $x=0$, we get\n$k_{1}(e^{0}) + k_{2}(\\sin 0) = 0; k_{1} \\cdot 1 + k_{2} \\cdot 0 = 0⇒k_{1} = 0$.\n- If $x=1$, we get\n$k_{1}(e^{1}) + k_{2}(\\sin 1) = 0; ek_{1} + (\\sin 1)k_{2} = 0⇒ek_{1} + (\\sin 1)k_{2} = 0$.\n\nThus, we obtain the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{k_{1} = 0; ek_{1} + (\\sin 1)k_{2} = 0\n$$\n\nThe only solution for this system is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{k_1=k_2=0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{all}}$ the scalar coefficients in the linear combination must be zero, the given set of functions is linearly $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{independent}}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fe4c8bb4-b7c0-5afc-86fd-40c20df9b953" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Pairs of Linearly Dependent and Linearly Independent Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9ff0b00-0ab0-5c40-b5b8-cf89a7c9292b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(− 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149733} {:answer/id #uuid "ccc78c3d-d536-521e-8dbb-e64f71dfd20c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149734} {:answer/id #uuid "4c9becfe-3668-5dec-8630-92fd0da695b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149735}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e9a41d70-ba03-5392-b35b-225c90a0af45" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149732} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c63d714e-9ef7-5db5-ab7b-4eefd3af892b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(− 1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149737} {:answer/id #uuid "560f219b-51ef-56ab-808c-9ec61367f23c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149738} {:answer/id #uuid "68347519-1d